{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2331 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9995a53d49fd8461aaec4e281bb1a724ee2f2af049657772eb377d0b2781d29d" :edb/db-before-t 2330 :edb/db-after-t 2331 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535643 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:21:17.772Z" 13194139535643 true] [17592186358936 1100 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of completed whole hours spent jogging during a week, ignoring any partial hour\n- The number of candies in a jar" 13194139535643 true] [17592186358936 1100 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of hours you spend jogging in a week\n- The number of candies in a jar" 13194139535643 false] [17592186358936 1145 "A discrete random variable has finitely many possible values or values that can be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement I measures elapsed time. The prompt does not restrict that duration to integer values, so it is a continuous random variable.\n- Statement II explicitly counts completed whole jogging hours. If the elapsed jogging time is $t$ hours, the recorded value is $\\lfloor t\\rfloor$. During one week, $0\\le t\\le168$, so the possible recorded values are the integers $0,1,2,\\ldots,168$. This is a discrete random variable.\n- Statement III counts candies. Its possible values are nonnegative integers, so it is a discrete random variable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nMeasuring elapsed time in hours alone does not make it discrete: fractional hours are possible. The explicit instruction to count completed whole hours makes Statement II discrete. The source explanation used integer hour counts; this wording states that convention directly." 13194139535643 true] [17592186358936 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535643 false]] :edb/tempids {}}
